import Native

{-
   The growable array - Design.md's "Collection types", written in the language over Native rather
   than generated by the compiler.

   **Every declaration that touches an array's storage is in `Array.native.yana` or
   `Array.js.yana`.** That is not an accident of where the split fell: the *owner* differs between
   the targets - natively a run of slots and a count, on JS the host's own array - so every
   operation over it does, down to `emptyArray`. Analysis-Modules.md §2.5 is the mechanism; this is
   the case where it goes all the way.

   `newArray` and the `Append` instance below it are the declarations that are here, and they are
   here precisely because they are the ones that do *not* differ: both are written over
   `emptyArray`, `reserve` and `push`, so both targets run the same body and a copy in each file
   would be the same lines twice.

   What is left is the part that is neither implementation: what an array *is*, why subscripting it
   is a generated instance rather than a conversion, and why the unclamped window exists. Two files
   answer the same names and a reader who wants to know which one runs reads the file name; a reader
   who wants to know what the names mean reads this.

   The classes those implementations join are `Container.yana`'s; there is no order between the
   files, because within a module a signature at the top of one may name a class at the bottom of
   another.

   What this is not, yet, is Implementation-Regions.md part 5's shared `Storage(a)` primitive with
   derived Drop. This is one collection with an authored Drop, which is the smaller thing that makes
   the storage decisions of Milestone 6 testable.
-}

{-
   What both files answer, and where the two differ in one line rather than in a body.

   `emptyArray` is an empty run natively and `[]` on JS, which is already the allocation - so the
   first `push` allocates on one target and never does on the other. `capacity` is the run's own
   number natively; a host array has none to report and answers what it holds, which is the honest
   answer for a container that never has to grow.
-}

{-
   Subscripting the owner - Core's `Index`.

   A second instance rather than the conversion alone, and the reason is generic code. `xs[i]`
   written against a concrete `Array(a)` could go on reaching `Index(Flat(a), Size, a)` through
   convertSlice, exactly as it always did; `fn (Index(c, k, v)) first(xs: c)` cannot, because an
   instance is selected by the type `c` *binds to* and a caller passing an array binds `Array(a)`.
   Instance selection does not convert - nothing about a class says which of its parameters may be
   widened on the way in - so an owner that is to be a `c` has to be one.

   **Generated rather than written, on the same terms the slice's are - see the note in `Native`.**
   Written out, the owner's pair would read:

       @platform(native) instance Index(Array(a), Size, a):
           fn get(return self: Array(a), index: Size) -> 'a = borrow(self.run.items + index)
           fn getMut(return &self: Array(a), index: Size) -> &a = borrowMut(self.run.items + index)

       @platform(js) instance Index(Array(a), Size, a):
           fn get(return self: Array(a), index: Size) -> 'a = hostAt(self.items, index)
           fn getMut(return &self: Array(a), index: Size) -> &a = hostAtMut(self.items, index)

   The bodies are the slice's, minus the descriptor. `self.run.items` is the base the slice would
   have copied, so this is one address computation rather than two loads and a temporary; the
   conversion was never buying anything at a subscript, and its absence is why the array's lowered
   form got shorter rather than longer when the class landed.

   On JS `hostAt` is a place rather than an operation, which is why one line is the whole of it:
   `arr[i]` is an lvalue in the host language exactly as `*(p + i)` is one here, so the borrow it
   hands back writes through to the array's own storage and no copy, box or write-back stands between
   them. That is what makes `xs[i] = v` on JS the same program it is natively rather than a read
   followed by a store somebody had to remember to emit.

   The count is not consulted, which is the same tier the slice's accessor is on and goes the same
   way when `checkBounds` (Implementation-Containers.md §15) lands - in one place, since by then
   there is one place.
-}

{-
   The same descriptor, for a window whose bounds are already known good.

   `slice` clamps both ends because a caller may write any two numbers, and every one of those clamps
   is a branch and a merge. A *whole* container is the case where none of them can fire - `from` is
   zero and `count` is the length the descriptor was read from - and the clamped form cannot be folded
   down to that: `start` and `end` are whole scalar locals, which opt_promote.cpp declines to promote
   by the rule in its header, so nothing at the resolve tier ever sees the two constants meet. The
   callee stayed seven blocks, the inliner sized it at seven blocks, and `elements` was a real call
   with a real frame in every vector loop in the language.

   So this is `slice` with the clamping taken out and the obligation moved to the caller, which is
   the only reason it is not `pub`: the two call sites in each platform file are the whole of it, and
   both pass the container's own length. `return self` for the same reason `slice` has it - the
   result points into whatever `self` points into.

   `count` rather than `to`, because a caller who has already established its bounds knows the length
   directly and subtracting a zero back off is the thing this exists to avoid.
-}

{-
   An array of `count` elements, each built from its index.

   The general form, and the one a fill is a special case of. A *fixed* array fills by writing one
   element at a type that holds more - `[0] :: [U8 *64]`, which is a literal and therefore a
   constant - and a growable one cannot, because nothing in `[Int]` states a count for the literal
   to spread into. So the count arrives as an argument here, and what fills each slot arrives as a
   function of the index rather than as a value: an array is not `TrivialCopy` in general, and a
   builder that took a value would have to duplicate it `count` times and could only ever serve the
   types where that is legal. `newArray(4, (i) -> emptyArray() :: Array(Int))` is four independent
   arrays, which is what a caller means and what one value repeated could not be.

   `reserve` before the loop rather than letting `push` grow: the length is known, so the doubling
   `push` would do - and the copy of everything written so far that comes with each of them - is the
   one cost this shape can remove without knowing anything about the element.

   The argument's type has to be written at the call - `newArray(4, (i: Size) -> ...)` - because
   `a` is still open where the lambda is resolved and the parameter's function type has nothing
   concrete to hand down. A named function passed directly needs no annotation.
-}
pub fn newArray(count: Size, item: (Size) -> a) -> Array(a):
    let &out = [] :: [a]
    reserve(out, count)

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        push(out, item(i))
        i = i + 1

    return out

{-
   Exchanging two elements of a window, as an operation the container offers.

   `swap(xs[i], xs[j])` is what a caller would write and it does not compile - test/resolve/
   Reject.Exchange.yana is where that is recorded, and the reason is an ordering rather than an
   omission: which accessor a subscript wants is decided by the parameter's convention, and the
   convention is not known until overload resolution has picked a callee, which needs the argument
   types. So an argument is resolved as an ordinary expression, reaches `get`, and arrives at the
   `&` parameter as a borrow that may not be written. That fixture's own comment names the way out -
   "a collection that wants an exchange of its own element offers an operation rather than leaving
   callers to write this" - and this is that operation.

   **Two derefs of the window's own base, rather than two calls to `getMut`.** The class route is
   not merely blocked at the subscript: `swap(getMut(xs, i), getMut(xs, j))` is two mutable borrows
   of one container alive at once, which is the case Reject.Ownership.yana exists for. The base is
   what neither of those can be taken of - a raw address is outside the ownership model by
   construction - so the exchange is written where the addresses are and nowhere above them.

   A swap and not a three-line rotate through a temporary, for the reason `swap` itself is a
   declaration rather than a body: `let temp = xs[i]` duplicates the element, which every element
   type that owns something forbids, and `let ->temp = xs[i]` leaves a hole in storage no local
   carries a state for. What both places hold before and after is a live value, which is exactly
   what `swap` promises and what makes this usable for a `String` and not only for an `Int`.

   Unchecked, on the same tier as `get`: both indices name live elements or the caller has already
   read past the end. `Sort.yana` is the one caller today and derives every index it passes from
   the window's own length.
-}

{-
   Joining two arrays - `Core`'s `Append`, and the second instance of it.

   **In the two platform files and not here**, which is a change from the borrowing version this
   replaces. That one sized a third array and copied both operands into it, so `emptyArray`,
   `reserve` and `push` were the whole seam and both targets answered all three. A consuming join
   takes the left operand's storage instead, and what that costs is target-shaped: natively the tail
   is one `copyMemory` of `n` strides, and on JS it is a read and a write per element because a host
   array's slots are not bytes anyone may move in bulk.

   **The element constraint is gone with it.** The borrowing join had to *duplicate* every element,
   so it carried `TrivialCopy(a)` and `Array(String)` had no `++` at all - the comment here used to
   say that an element type owning something has no join and that a caller should write the pushes
   out by hand. Moving an element is something every type permits, so the instance is now
   unconstrained and `[String] ++ [String]` is an ordinary sentence.

   The one thing both bodies must do is empty the donor before it dies. Its elements belong to the
   result now, and `Reclaim(Array(a))`'s walk is over the live prefix - so a donor left at its old
   length would run a teardown on every element the result is still holding.
-}

-- And the identity, which makes `Array(a)` a `Monoid` and every fold in Join.yana available on it.
-- Unconstrained like the join above: an empty array of anything is an empty run and a zero, whatever
-- the element would have been.
instance Monoid(Array(a)):
    fn empty() -> Array(a) = emptyArray()

{-
   Duplicating one - `Core`'s `Copy`.

   An array owns its run, so it is not TrivialCopy whatever its elements are and the blanket instance
   beside the class does not reach it. `Copy(a)` on the element and not `TrivialCopy(a)`: an element
   that is itself an owner has a `Copy` of its own - `String` does, and so does a nested `Array` by
   this instance - and the blanket makes every trivial element satisfy it too, so the one constraint
   covers both worlds. `copy` on an `Int` is the identity function and disappears.

   A plain function with the instance forwarding to it, for the reason `joinedArrays` is one: a
   constraint may only be written in a signature, and a class function's signature is the class's.

   Element-wise and not one block copy, because that is what a `Copy(a)` element means - a type whose
   bytes are not its whole story is exactly the case `Copy` exists for. Where the element *is*
   trivial the loop body is the copy alone and nothing is called, which is the shape the backend can
   see through; a bulk path for that row would need an instance per constraint, and two instances at
   `Array(a)` differing only in what they demand of `a` overlap.
-}
fn (Copy(a)) copiedArray(from: Array(a)) -> Array(a):
    let count = length(from)

    let &out = [] :: [a]
    reserve(out, count)

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        let ->item = copy(from[i])
        push(out, item)
        i = i + 1

    return out

instance (Copy(a)) Copy(Array(a)):
    fn copy(from: Array(a)) -> Array(a) = copiedArray(from)
